        "session_id": "82a2b769-2018-45c0-9d5e-0522467333ad",
        "user_prompt": "What is OpenID Connect OIDC Identity Federation?",
        "final_response": "OpenID Connect (OIDC) Identity Federation is a model used in modern pipelines to secure how Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) platforms authenticate with public cloud providers. This approach allows the CI/CD platform to gain access to the cloud provider without relying on traditional, insecure static credentials like long-lived access keys.\n\nThis identity federation model addresses security challenges by enabling dynamic authentication during execution. The process involves a flow where the CI/CD runner requests an OpenID Connect Token (JWT) from the runner provider. This token is then presented to the cloud provider's Security Token Service (STS) using the web identity assume-role API for validation.\n\nDuring this process, the Cloud STS validates the JWT's signature and evaluates identity conditions defined on the destination IAM role. If the checks pass, STS issues short-lived, scoped credentials to the runner, which are then used to deploy resources.\n\n- Citation: Page 4, Source docs\\CICD_ResearchIntroduction.pdf"
    },
